This paper investigates the existence of standardized ground states for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, $\mu$, and the nonhomogeneous term $f$, and proves the standard minimizer existence for a modified Choquard type equation with exponential critical expansion through a variational approach. Some conditions on the fractional operator and the critical growth of the function are also analyzed. Moreover, this work extends previous research on standardized ground states for the Choquard equation to a broader class of domains and expands the scope of the exponential critical expansion to $n$-dimensional spaces. Some comments on further potential research are included. Additionally, a related noncompact imbedding result in weighted Sobolev spaces has a proof appended in the appendix. This includes a comparison with a related result in Ref. [19]. Furthermore, this work's extensions on both the domain and the exponential critical expansion of the fractional Choquard equation for $n$-$\mu$ spaces have been posted on the preprint server arXiv for reference. 

